The `linkable` filter (lines 282-287) decides whether a root-authored patch is a genuine chain extension:

```ts
const linkable = rootAuthored.filter((p) => {
  const parentId = replyTarget(p)
  if (parentId === undefined) return false
  const parent = byId.get(parentId)
  return parent === undefined || parent.pubkey === root.pubkey
})
```

The `parent === undefined` disjunct (parent event not present anywhere in the input `events` array) optimistically counts the patch as linkable. This clause is never exercised by any test: `_resolve-generators.ts`'s scenario generator only ever wires root-authored patches to *previously generated, always-observed* parents (`parent = idAt[i-1]` style), and its only 'dangling reference' generator (`danglingOverlays`) produces *foreign* patches with an unresolved `e reply`, never a root-authored one. `resolve.test.ts` and the vendored `vectors/application.json` corpus (checked directly — 46 `chain/*` cases) have no case for a root-authored patch whose reply target is absent from the observed set while the root itself is present; the only 'unobserved' vector (`chain/patch-whose-root-is-unobserved-is-retained`) is the different UR-2 case (root itself missing).

More importantly, I worked through why this branch is reachable-but-inert for a single `resolve()` call: `byId` is built from the exact same `events` array that supplies `patches`/`root`, so if `parentId` is not in `byId`, it can never equal a walk `position` (positions are always `rootId` or the id of a previously-walked, already-observed patch). Whether such a patch is marked `linkable: true` or `false`, it ends up sitting in the `children` map under a key (`parentId`) that the walk will never visit in *this* call, so `chainPatches`, `fork`, `applied`, and all overlay classifications are provably identical either way. That makes the clause either (a) dead code that can be deleted without changing any observable `resolve()` output (the `false` branch alone is enough, since `parent === undefined` already implies exclusion has no effect), or (b) load-bearing for some interaction I have not found — and given it is completely untested, nobody currently knows which. Either the branch should get a property/example test that actually distinguishes it from `false`, or (if my reachability argument holds) it should be simplified to `return parent !== undefined && parent.pubkey === root.pubkey`, removing an unexercised special case entirely.
